<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.7.2: Finding Slope From Tables, Graphs, and Points</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00963</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.7.2: Finding Slope From Tables, Graphs, and Points</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4d9af850-e378-4aea-b48f-1975c85c636b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Recall that the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">slope</term> of a line is the rate of change of the linear function. On the graph, it is the steepness of the line.</para>

<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="431b54f0-b83b-4912-bcc2-9bda8cb5d327"><problem><para>
      <para> Find the slope of the line that connects \((0,0)\) and \((3,2)\)</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>2/3</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is \(\frac23\)</para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
</content>
</document>
